-- X13 (#2185, S0, §4b LICENSED FLIP) x12's NO-IMPL sibling: the SAME `Ifc`
-- interface declares a method literally named `println`, but NO impl exists
-- anywhere in the program. The unimplemented interface is otherwise wholly
-- irrelevant -- nothing ever calls it (the same "declared and never called
-- costs nothing" shape x6 pins for the ordinary user-occurrence case) -- so
-- the wrap's synthesized reference must be unaffected by its mere existence.
--
-- MEASURED ON THE BASE BINARY (93a40382, cold-built in this worktree,
-- MEDAKA_STRICT=1): check/run/build ALL REJECT (exit 1), `No impl of Ifc for
-- Int`, order-invariant -- the wrap's synthesized `println` was marked as a
-- dispatch against THIS module's `Ifc`, found no impl at Int, and the whole
-- program that never once writes the word `println` failed to build over an
-- interface it never uses.
--
-- FIXED shape (this slice, Val-licensed 2026-08-30 to flip reject -> accept):
-- the wrap no longer spells the name `println` (`putStrLn (display <e>)`), so
-- it cannot route through `Ifc` at all -- check/run/build must ALL ACCEPT and
-- the built binary must print 1, the value `main` actually holds. `run`
-- REJECTs BY DESIGN regardless (#1681, mirrors x7/x11/x12: `main` is a
-- non-Unit VALUE and `medaka run` never applies or prints one) -- graded
-- BUILD_EXACT for the same reason. Value hand-derived from the literal
-- `main = 1`, not captured.
interface Ifc c where
  println : c -> Int

main = 1
